<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sanskritisation</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sanskritization</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sanskritisation is the process by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> and tribal groups in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> adopt the customs, rituals, values and way of life of the dominant upper castes in order to rise in the caste hierarchy. The term was popularised in the 1950s by the sociologist M. N. Srinivas, who first set out the idea in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> thesis on the Kodava of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, showing that a caste could rise within a generation or two by taking up vegetarianism, teetotalism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> rites. His work challenged the view of caste as rigid and unchanging. It has been observed especially among mid-ranked castes, as when pastoral communities such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahir">Ahir</a> took the name Yadav in the late 19th and early 20th centuries.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Differences between texts of the canon suggest material from more than one dialect, and after its first compromise form the language underwent a small degree of <b>Sanskritisation</b>, as when Middle Indic bamhana gave way to brāhmana.</blockquote><a href="/language/pali">Pali · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote>Some late medieval authors resisted <b>Sanskritisation</b>, and in the 20th century the Pure Tamil Movement, taṉit tamiḻ iyakkam, led by Parithimaar Kalaignar and Maraimalai Adigal and supported by Dravidian parties, replaced many Sanskrit words; by some estimates their share in formal Tamil fell from 40–50% to about 20%.</blockquote><a href="/language/tamil">Tamil · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Lower castes sought Brahmin or Kshatriya status for whole communities, a process called <b>Sanskritisation</b>; the upper castes had resisted such claims before British rule, but they spread under it.</blockquote><a href="/societies/british-raj">Social Hierarchy of British India</a></li></ul></section>
